  "result": "`summarize()` now handles 200,000 orders in about 46 ms, down from minutes. Its output is unchanged, including ordering, and `npm test` passes (4 of 4).\n\nWhy it was slow, and the fix in `src/report.js`:\n- **Duplicate check:** it scanned an array for every order. It now uses a `Set`.\n- **Customer lookup:** it scanned a list of groups for every order. It now uses a `Map`.\n- **Sorting:** it re-sorted the whole leaderboard after every order. It now sorts once at the end. The sort is stable, so ties still come out in the same order as before.\n- **NaN customer IDs:** the old code never matched a `NaN` `customerId` to an existing group, so each such order made its own group. I kept that behaviour explicitly.\n\nTwo tests added in `test/report.test.js`:\n- **Behaviour check:** it copies the original slow algorithm and compares its output with the new one using `deepStrictEqual`. It runs on 20 seeded random datasets with duplicate order IDs, refunds, EUR orders and tied ranks.\n- **Speed check:** it times 200,000 orders and fails if `summarize()` takes 1 second or more.\n\nI did not run the new code against real production data, only the random test data and a 200,000-order synthetic timing run.",
